#{[17592186111680 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1061 476192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "888ce267-7f48-5074-a1ab-42caf954a38c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1061 476193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f94c8cb-7345-56bc-b884-f0ac3f242910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1061 476194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e04eac3-4a93-5d72-9a90-2b1099628885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1061 476197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d109dfd-fdee-5718-a3d2-a9a96f41ffd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1061 476198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01172b4e-9bb6-512f-9e14-1b2ad76e2a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1061 476199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8fdb7d5-63c2-55ac-a293-d9536babfe47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1061 8758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8e313bb-3f6b-5512-9eaa-d26017f922aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1061 8768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8bbc417-4a3f-577b-991f-4a7306fb343e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1061 8772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ad48197-e2e5-58b1-af58-7632d0bd7fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1061 8773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a60a9ec-5f67-5382-8520-b964b945c556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1064 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1065 11389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "edb6ae26-e191-5d7d-9ce8-ad9ee36655f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1065 11486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d28dc5e-68de-53d0-a57c-9072a89eccbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1065 13403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1065 13429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8428afc7-2e9a-55d0-9f13-7a2acdf8fd7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1064 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1065 13430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ,$\n\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ,$\n\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ,$\n\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ,$ and\n\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ.$\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1065 2182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "282e8601-d3f4-5c34-a96b-a071a96de244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111914 #edb/ref 1064 "A biologist is experimenting with bacteria stored in a Petri dish. She observes that the number of bacteria in the dish *triples* every hour. If there were $100$ bacteria initially, can she determine how many bacteria will be present after $3$ hours?\n\nStarting with $100$ bacteria, she knows that this number *triples* after one hour. Remember that tripling means to multiply by a factor of $3$.\n\nTo get the number of bacteria after one hour, we multiply the initial amount by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}{100\\cdot 3}\n$$\n\nAfter another hour ($2$ hours in total), the number above triples again. So to get the total number of bacteria after $2$ hours, we multiply the amount above by $3$:\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{100\\cdot 3}})\\cdot 3 = {\\color{red}{100 \\cdot 3^2}}\n$$\n\nAnd after another hour ($3$ hours total), the number above triples once again:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{100 \\cdot 3^2}})\\cdot 3 = 100 \\cdot 3^3\n$$\n\nIf we evaluate the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n100 \\cdot 3^3 = 100\\cdot 27 = 2\\,700\n$$\n\nSo after $3$ hours, there will be $2\\,700$ bacteria in the Petri dish.\n\nThis is an example of **exponential growth**. The number of bacteria triples over a time interval of $1$ hour, every hour. The total number of bacteria grows very rapidly, as we can see from the chart below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf039743a936cab0ca74708584fc3670202588d15fc63a4257ad1a737047da2d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111914 #edb/ref 1065 8841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111914 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60dc9833-8054-5a01-9e29-d592d2e4498a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111914 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1039 "Isolating an Absolute Value Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192634 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5bc2c576-4f7c-5b4a-8c78-8a41420fdcce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1039 "Absolute Value Equations With No Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192635 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "94d6a2aa-4288-51dc-8944-cf9490812ad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1039 "Rearranging and Solving an Absolute Value Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192636 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "15f03573-42ee-50d4-acdf-e2ee05d3e7e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Absolute Value Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192907 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "157f57f7-71ad-5178-b6a1-cb1065e17520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193425 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193425 #edb/ref 1061 144049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193425 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4b80cf6-ac66-5b80-8e9a-84e34ac2fd5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193425 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193426 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193426 #edb/ref 1061 144050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193426 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e54dac3-7257-553a-9281-d652e78869f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193426 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193427 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193427 #edb/ref 1061 144054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193427 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f7f7690-789e-59b6-a31a-f699646aed1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193427 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193428 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193428 #edb/ref 1061 144055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193428 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd2c5b77-7fcb-5443-969e-13f565428281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193428 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193429 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193429 #edb/ref 1061 144056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193429 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc568d38-8ed7-5e05-b47d-35fcd03f33e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193429 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193430 #edb/ref 1061 144059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193430 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9aebc5c1-cf53-5a78-8700-b622cd65203b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193430 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193943 #edb/ref 1064 "A valuable skill to master when dealing with absolute value equations is to isolate the absolute value expression first.\n\nTo isolate an absolute value expression, we treat it like a variable in a linear equation.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n2|x-1| + 4 = 8\n$$\n\nWe aim to isolate the $|x-1|$ term on the left-hand side. \n\nWe start by subtracting $4$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x-1|+4 & =8 \\\\ 2|x-1|+4-4 & =8-4 \\\\ 2|x-1| & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x-1| & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2|x-1|}{2} & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}|x-1|}{\\cancel{2}} & =2 \\\\ |x-1| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We've successfully isolated the absolute value expression in our equation.\n\nLet's consider another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193943 #edb/ref 1065 8824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193943 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "640fe647-93cb-5b1a-8707-6c53032d81ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193943 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193944 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\vert x \\vert = -4\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The absolute value of any number is *always* positive or zero.\n\n- So, there is no number whose absolute value is negative.\n\n- Therefore, this equation $|x| = -4$ has **no solution.**\n\nAnother way of understanding this is to recall that the absolute value of a number represents the number's distance from the origin, and distances cannot be negative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193944 #edb/ref 1065 8826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193944 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b272858e-f85c-5daa-ad70-c87f7a3b792f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193944 #edb/ref 1067 "Recognizing Equations With No Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193945 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we know that the following equation has no solution:\n\n$$\n\\vert x \\vert = -4\n$$\n\nLet's see what happens when we blindly apply the usual process of solving our equation:\n\n- if $|x| = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x| & =-4 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- if $|x| = -x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x| & =-4 \\\\ -x & =-4 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the usual process has given us the \"solutions\" $x= {\\color{red}{-4}}$ and $x= {\\color{blue}{4}}.$ However, neither of these values satisfies the original equation!\n\nFor example, by substituting $x = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ into the original equation, we get a *false statement!*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x| & =-4 \\\\ |4| & =-4 \\\\ 4 & =-4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway is that it's essential to check that the right-hand side is positive (or zero) *before* applying the usual solution process." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193945 #edb/ref 1065 8827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193945 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea3bc553-4605-5ceb-8f0b-e788bb67414a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193945 #edb/ref 1067 "Attempting to Solve an Equation With No Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295383 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295530 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d6f283f-3592-5248-baf7-ca782b7d8c9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295633 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4009ef03-e229-5324-9dbd-214305b60cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295715 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295715 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295715 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4dfca85a-c698-5e36-ba67-02a55f783f85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296371 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296371 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296371 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d199324f-9733-59f2-82cf-8e4f45ae01ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296621 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186295383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1739b830-13d4-586f-9761-4372557e83e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186296621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "58355121-2d5d-5aa8-91c2-79f41befa005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476103 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186110719.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476103 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "393aec7c-08a3-58cd-ac4d-3705704af62b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476187 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(6p-6)x=36x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c6ffd8f-aed0-5462-a678-623c3eb10c91" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=6p-6,\\,b=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n6p-6=36.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6p & =42 \\\\ p & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476194 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5p-3)x=37x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8af6ded4-48a3-5261-b74f-acd5512ac6e3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p-3,\\,b=37.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p-3=37.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p & =40 \\\\ p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476181 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(3p-1)x=17x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0e66191-a3c9-5572-8227-0f5cb2926471" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=3p-1,\\,b=17.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n3p-1=17.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =18 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476178 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(7p-1)x=20x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2e51c73-6455-54ee-866f-56127d07e55b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p-1,\\,b=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p-1=20.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p & =21 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476195 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7px=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e320282a-d753-5114-aed7-fb576099a4d8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476197 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186110720.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476197 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "c05f43b0-71c3-5af5-bc14-8a813a2da1af" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477025 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "436cab40-c84e-51f6-a518-ef754e1d23e8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+7=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=88.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=2$ and $n=11,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=2+11=13.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477019 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "475d1245-f209-5a1a-94d0-b61255568fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+16=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=9.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=9$ and $t=6,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=9+6=15.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477015 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af3151d7-d56f-5c03-a92e-cf11481af995" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+15=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=12.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n4t=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=12$ and $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=12+1=13.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477004 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bae891b2-2044-5361-b2ef-88211b0b3d9f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+8=19.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=48.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=11$ and $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=11+6=17.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477010 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [175921864